---
term: Obscured consensus
section: premise
order: 30
definition: Agreement that forms implicitly through side channels and private deals, which can settle into a worse outcome than open bargaining because each party's position stays hidden.
example: Several cities along a river each make private water deals with their neighbors. Each deal is reasonable on its own, but no city sees the full picture. The river runs low in dry years, even though a shared allocation would serve every city better.
seeAlso:
  - action-situation
  - question-of-arbitration
---
When positions are hidden, the party with better information gains at the others' expense, and good outcomes get crowded out. A stock market without disclosure rules shows the pattern: manipulation thrives where investors can't see what others know. Mandatory disclosure, enforced by a regulator, corrects it.

Side channels are also costly. A group of *n* parties that coordinates through bilateral deals needs up to *n*(*n* − 1)/2 separate agreements, and a single forum needs one. In a network of private deals, no party knows what every other party has agreed to, and coordination depends on that shared knowledge. Game theorists call it *common knowledge*: everyone knows a fact, everyone knows that everyone knows it, and so on. Michael Chwe shows that public events and forums exist largely to create it (*Rational Ritual*, 2001).

The Social Charter replaces obscured consensus with explicit charters. Each charter makes the problem explicit: its parties, scope, and rules. Whether members' positions are public or sealed is a choice for its mechanism. A single assembly of nations works the same way: it decides little on its own, but it makes coordination possible where a hundred private conversations don't.
